    description: "Successor umbrella to `WI-nigah-hubuj-mobuh-zurus-limab-sonol-jutiz-kumuh` (closed `done` 2026-05-31). WI-nigah's Tier 1 (groovy) + Tier 2 (lua / tcl / objc / perl / powershell) shipped 2026-05-31; this item carries the remaining outstanding work, in two parts.\n\n## Part 1 — Tier 3: lang-extended1 long-tail sweep\n\nPer-analyzer retrofit of `make_unresolved_edge` call sites in `hypergumbo-lang-extended1`, prioritized by unresolved-call-edge volume on the WI-kaziv 6-repo cohort where data exists. Apply the WI-huzuv / WI-nigah Tier 2 pattern: extract a `module_path` signal from the call-site syntax (alias dict, namespace separator, package separator, or convention-based receiver heuristic), populate `dst_ref=ExternalRef(...)`, keep `dst` and `dst_ref` coherent.\n\n**Data-driven priority:**\n\n- **d_lang** — 1121 unresolved/repo on the cohort. Mechanism: D uses `import` statements and `.`-separated module paths. Highest single-language priority in Tier 3.\n- **pascal** — 73 unresolved/repo on the cohort. Mechanism: Pascal `uses` clause; less qualified-call syntax than D but worth a per-analyzer look.\n\n**Audit-pending (14 analyzers — no data, no investigation):**\n\nFor each of these I have *not* opened the analyzer file or measured anything. The required audit per language is:\n\n1. Does the analyzer have a `make_unresolved_edge` call site at all?\n2. What import / namespace / aliasing mechanism does the language use?\n3. What does the analyzer emit (unresolved-edge volume on a representative repo)?\n\nPer-language audit-pending list: `ada`, `asm`, `cobol`, `fennel`, `fish`, `gdscript`, `gleam`, `haxe`, `janet`, `nim`, `odin`, `pony`, `solidity`, `v_lang`.\n\nSome of these (cobol, asm) likely emit zero call edges in practice and may close `wont_do` after audit. Others (gdscript, nim, solidity) probably have meaningful signal.\n\n## Part 2 — WI-zisov polyglot-fixture xfail gap-closure\n\nPR #3995 added 5 new polyglot fixtures (kotlin, scala, php, swift, csharp) with strict `xfail_reason` markers documenting analyzer gaps discovered at audit time. Closing each gap removes the marker and surfaces the fix as XPASSED.\n\n- **kotlin** — `Item.create()` (qualified call via imported type) emits no edge. The analyzer treats imported-type qualified calls as a different resolution path than bare-named imports. Retrofit shape: extend the `var_types` / `import_aliases` tracking to also resolve `Type.method()` patterns to the imported module.\n- **scala** — `Item.create()` (qualified call via imported object) emits the `external` sentinel for `module_hint` instead of threading the import-tracker output. Retrofit shape: thread imports through to the qualified-call emit site, similar to the kotlin gap but with Scala's object-vs-class semantics.\n- **php** — `Item::create()` (static-method via `use`-imported class) emits no edge. The analyzer doesn't currently dispatch `::` static-method calls through the `use` registry. Retrofit shape: detect `::` static-call construct and look up the receiver in the `use` map.\n- **csharp** — emits **no** unresolved external-call edges at all. The analyzer only resolves local methods. Needs WI-mafik-style retrofit from scratch: build import tracking (`using` directives + `using static`), wire `make_unresolved_edge` into the call-emit path, populate `dst_ref`. Largest piece of work in this item.\n\nEach of the four xfail markers in `test_polyglot_call_site_coverage.py` cites this WI-nigah successor as the gap-closure home (those citations were written when the umbrella was still WI-nigah; they remain accurate pointers to the successor).\n\n## Out of scope (inherited from WI-nigah)\n\n- Tooling languages with no call-edge concept (gitignore, ini, properties, yaml_ansible, dockerfile, manifest_targets, json_config, xml_config, toml_config, requirements, jvm_deps).\n- def_use companion modules (py_def_use, rust_def_use, ts_def_use).\n- Pure SCIP / IR-backed analyzers (rust_scip).\n- Validation / retrofit of the analyzers already shipped — those are closed under WI-mafik (java/go/elixir/js_ts/cpp/rust/ruby), WI-huzuv (kotlin/php/scala/swift mechanical sweep), and WI-nigah Tier 1+2 (groovy/lua/tcl/objc/perl/powershell). The WI-zisov xfail gaps for kotlin/scala/php are NEW work because they target a different construct (qualified-call paths) than the mechanical sweep covered.\n\n## Trigger condition for promotion to `todo_hard`\n\nPromote Part 1 d_lang from this umbrella to a standalone `todo_hard` item when next prioritized — the volume signal (1121/repo) is the highest in Tier 3 and warrants direct attention rather than umbrella tracking.\n\nPromote Part 2 csharp similarly: it's the largest single piece of work in this umbrella (analyzer infrastructure from scratch) and probably wants its own item with a clearer scope statement."  # ab12
